CloudBurrow patch 108 to github.com/goccy/go-googlesql v0.3.0
(cloudburrow/cloudburrow#2134).

The bug: a generated method copies the C++ pointers of its receiver and
handle arguments into its request, then calls invokeMethod, which waits
for the module's lock. A handle whose last Go use was that copy could be
finalized, and its C++ object freed in the engine's WebAssembly memory,
before the call read it. On main CI (run 44954090598) googlesqlite's
newSimpleCatalog passed its LanguageOptions inside BuiltinFunctionOptions
to AddGoogleSQLFunctions2, which then spun forever in the analyzer.

The fix is not in this patch: it is tools/bqengine's handle-keepalive
rewrite of googlesql.go (sources.json "rewrites", keepalive.go), run after
the patches, which changes every generated method too wide a change for a
patch. Every method keeps its receiver and each argument that can hold a
handle (an options struct keeps the handles in it) alive until its call
has returned, and each finalizer is on the struct that holds the C++
pointer, which a base class's method has as its receiver.

This patch adds two test hooks to googlesql.go, testHookBeforeCall (in
invokeMethod, after marshalling and before the call) and testHookInvoke
(every request, frees included), and keepalive_test.go, which forces a
collection and runs the pending finalizers in that gap, and fails if the
handle's free reached the module before the call: for a receiver, a base
class's receiver, an argument and a handle in an options struct. All four
fail without the rewrite; the base class's also with the KeepAlives alone.

